Can you explain what this code does?

function debounce(fn, wait) {
  let timer;
  return function (...args) {
    clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = setTimeout(() => fn.apply(this, args), wait);
  };
}

const onResize = debounce(() => {
  console.log('resized');
}, 200);

window.addEventListener('resize', onResize);

I see this pattern a lot in tutorials and I want to understand why the timer is cleared each time, what 'this' refers to inside the returned function, and whether there is a simpler way to write it with modern JavaScript. I have been learning JavaScript for about three months, mostly from free online courses, and closures still confuse me sometimes, especially when they are combined with timers and event listeners like in this example here.
